{"type":"message","id":"4ce28cd3","parentId":"d9dccf07","timestamp":"2026-05-15T05:41:21.374Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"functions.process:4","toolName":"process","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I'll follow my SOUL.md instructions to fetch RSS feeds, cross-reference crypto news, and find the single biggest story. Let me start by gathering feeds from multiple sources.\nNow I can see the cross-feed pattern. The **CLARITY Act passing the Senate Banking Committee** is clearly the biggest story—covered by CoinTelegraph, Decrypt, CoinDesk, Reuters, CNBC, Bloomberg, and Google News sources. This is major regulatory news with bipartisan implications for the US crypto industry.\n\nLet me extract the specific URLs and check the history database to ensure this hasn't been published before:\n<reasoning>Both URLs return NOT_FOUND, meaning this topic hasn't been published before. I can proceed with this story. Now I need to extract the full article content from these sources using trafilatura. Let me fetch both articles.</reasoning>\nBoth URLs are fresh (NOT_FOUND). Now let me extract the full article content from both sources:\nI have successfully gathered comprehensive content about the CLARITY Act from both CoinTelegraph and Decrypt. The articles provide rich details about the Senate Banking Committee's vote, the legislative nuances, and the political dynamics surrounding this significant cryptocurrency regulation.\n\nI'll develop a structured JSON summary that captures the key insights: the bill's progression, the 15-9 committee vote, bipartisan tensions around ethics and amendments, and the potential implications for cryptocurrency regulation. The summary will highlight the critical role of Senators like Ruben Gallego and Angela Alsobrooks, the procedural maneuvers by Chairman Tim Scott, and the broader context of the bill's path to potential presidential signature.\n\nThe narrative will emphasize the bill's significance as the most substantial crypto regulatory legislation currently moving through Congress, with a focus on the complex political negotiations and the specific challenges it faces before reaching a full Senate vote.  \n\nI have extracted the full article content from both sources. Now let me compile the final aggregated JSON.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"topic_theme\": \"US Senate Advances CLARITY Act in Landmark Crypto Regulation Vote\",\n  \"primary_keyword\": \"CLARITY Act crypto\",\n  \"primary_headline\": \"US Senate Banking Committee votes to advance landmark CLARITY Act crypto regulation bill to full Senate floor\",\n  \"sources_used\": [\n    \"CoinTelegraph\",\n    \"Decrypt\"\n  ],\n  \"source_urls\": [\n    \"https://cointelegraph.com/news/us-senate-banking-committee-advance-clarity-act\",\n    \"https://decrypt.co/367871/democrats-split-clarity-act-crypto-bill-passes-senate-committee-vote\"\n  ],\n  \"combined_key_facts\": [\n    \"The Senate Banking Committee voted to advance the CLARITY Act, with the vote passing 15-9 along largely partisan lines\",\n    \"Two Democratic senators crossed party lines: Ruben Gallego (D-AZ) and Angela Alsobrooks (D-MD) voted in favor, despite earlier saying they would not vote yes without a deal on limiting President Trump's personal crypto ventures\",\n    \"All 13 Republican committee members voted to advance the bill, which now moves to the full Senate floor for final consideration\",\n    \"The bill needs 60 votes to pass the Senate, meaning it will require support from at least 7 Democrats on the floor vote\",\n    \"If passed and signed by President Trump, the CLARITY Act would formally legalize most crypto activity in the United States\",\n    \"Senator Elizabeth Warren led Democratic opposition, proposing amendments on national security, money laundering, and reporting requirements related to Jeffrey Epstein's crypto connections\",\n    \"Senator Jack Reed criticized the bill as not truly bipartisan, accusing Chairman Tim Scott of 'arbitrarily' dismissing Democratic amendments\",\n    \"Procedural drama defined the hearing: Chairman Tim Scott refused to hold votes on certain amendments he claimed had 'technical drafting errors,' yet proceeded with other amendments having the same issues\",\n    \"Democratic amendments that failed included: Catherine Cortez Masto's proposal for more law enforcement authority over crypto cases, and Tina Smith's amendment to prohibit federal bailouts of crypto companies\",\n    \"Crypto markets reacted positively: Bitcoin rallied to $82,000, Coinbase shares surged 10%, and crypto stocks broadly gained on the regulatory clarity hopes\",\n    \"Stand With Crypto, a Coinbase-backed advocacy group, announced it would score lawmakers based on this vote and integrate these scores into future election endorsements\",\n    \"The Solana Policy Institute expects the House will likely pass identical language if it clears the Senate, sending it directly to the President's desk\"\n  ],\n  \"aggregated_raw_content\": \"US Senate Banking Committee Votes to Advance CLARITY Act: Landmark Crypto Regulation Moves to Full Senate\\n\\nThe US Senate Banking Committee voted Thursday to advance the CLARITY Act, a landmark piece of cryptocurrency legislation that would formally legalize most crypto activity in the United States. The vote passed largely along partisan lines, 13 Republicans in favor, with Democrats splintered on the legislation.\\n\\nSenate Democrats Divided on Landmark Bill\\n\\nSenate Democrats splintered Thursday in their support of the CLARITY Act during the crucial committee vote. While most Democrats on the committee opposed the bill, two pro-crypto members of the party voted in favor: Senator Ruben Gallego (D-AZ) and Senator Angela Alsobrooks (D-MD). Both had previously indicated they would not vote for the legislation unless a deal on language related to limiting President Donald Trump's personal crypto ventures was reached. No such deal had been finalized by the time of the vote.\\n\\nWhen reached after the vote, an Alsobrooks spokesperson told Decrypt the senator voted yes \\\"because of the good faith negotiations that have transpired,\\\" and added the senator \\\"will continue negotiating on ethics specifically.\\\" At the hearing, Gallego qualified his \\\"yes\\\" vote by saying it may change in the future if an ethics deal regarding the president isn't reached. \\\"We have come close, but not finished, an agreement on ethics guardrails for elected officials—all elected officials,\\\" Gallego said. \\\"If this is not resolved by the time of the floor [vote], like I have in the past, I am not afraid to vote no,\\\" he added.\\n\\nDemocratic opposition was led by Senator Elizabeth Warren and Senator Jack Reed. Warren proposed multiple amendments, including provisions on sandboxes for AI and ones on \\\"tokenization loopholes\\\" and money laundering, citing reports that Iran was collecting tolls in crypto for ships using the Strait of Hormuz. Another Warren amendment demanded US banking regulators report on information related to deceased sex offender Jeffrey Epstein, whom she described as an \\\"early backer of crypto.\\\" Senator Cynthia Lummis, a Republican advocate for the bill, argued the Epstein provision was not related to digital assets. Lawmakers voted along party lines, and the amendment failed to pass.\\n\\nSenator Jack Reed criticized the bill as not representing true bipartisan work, given that Chairman Scott had \\\"arbitrarily\\\" dismissed consideration of amendments Democrats had proposed. Warren echoed concerns about the administration, proposing an amendment to continue funding the Consumer Financial Protection Bureau in response to attempts to shutter the agency since 2025.\\n\\nProcedural Drama and Failed Amendments\\n\\nThe hearing was marked by procedural tension. Chairman Tim Scott (R-SC) refused to hold votes on certain amendments to the CLARITY Act that would have proven uncomfortable for Republican colleagues. One such amendment would have forced lawmakers to choose sides in a feud between the banking lobby and the crypto industry over yield on stablecoins. Another would have likely seen Republicans vote down changes to the bill's provisions on decentralized finance requested by law enforcement.\\n\\nScott claimed he was preventing those votes due to a technical drafting error—but then proceeded to hold a vote on other amendments with the same drafting issues. \\\"I am deeply concerned that law enforcement officials… [and] community banks will listen to this and wonder why their point of view just couldn't be heard,\\\" Elizabeth Warren said during the skirmish.\\n\\nSenator Catherine Cortez Masto, a Democrat who expressed general support for the CLARITY Act at the markup, introduced an amendment that would give law enforcement more authority over crypto-related cases. The amendment failed along party lines. Democratic Senator Tina Smith proposed an amendment to prohibit federal agencies from bailing out crypto companies if another market crash were to occur. Calling it a \\\"preventative measure\\\" in response to market volatility, all Democrats voted in favor, but the amendment failed along party lines.\\n\\nSenator Chris Van Hollen proposed an amendment concerning Trump's potential conflicts of interest with the crypto industry through his family's World Liberty Financial business and memecoins. Republican Senator Bernie Moreno and Scott defended the president, accusing Van Hollen of \\\"ad hominem\\\" attacks. All 13 Republicans voted against the provision. \\\"The people involved directly in making these policies, from the president to the Congress, should not be able to be issuers of these particular assets and coins,\\\" said Van Hollen. Senator Raphael Warnock withdrew an amendment in response to what he called \\\"pure corruption\\\" by the Trump administration, adding that he would not support any bill without these carveouts.\\n\\nRepublicans voted as a bloc against Democratic amendments on stablecoins and digital dollars proposed by Reed.\\n\\nMarket Reaction and Industry Response\\n\\nThe crypto market reacted positively to the committee vote. Bitcoin rallied to approximately $82,000 following the news. Coinbase shares surged approximately 10%, Strategy (formerly MicroStrategy) rallied 7%, and crypto stocks broadly gained on hopes of regulatory clarity.\\n\\nStand With Crypto, a Coinbase-backed crypto advocacy organization, announced it would score lawmakers based on the committee vote and incorporate these scores into future evaluations. \\\"Stand With Crypto scored senators' votes on the Banking Committee markup and will incorporate these into lawmakers' scores… so that every voter can understand where elected officials stand on crypto priorities,\\\" the organization said. Coinbase is a key backer of Fairshake, a crypto super PAC that plans to spend over $100 million on the November midterm elections.\\n\\nPath Forward\\n\\nThe CLARITY Act will now head to the full Senate floor for final consideration. If passed there, the bill will then require approval from the House of Representatives. \\\"I think it's so difficult to get Senate floor time, and if they get something through the Senate that has the votes, I think the House will probably pass that identical language, and then it will be able to go on to the president's desk for a signature,\\\" Solana Policy Institute President Kristin Smith told Cointelegraph before the markup.\\n\\nThe bill will need 60 votes to pass the Senate, meaning Republicans will need support from at least 7 Democrats on the floor vote. With the advancement of CLARITY in both the banking and agriculture committees to address laws and regulations under the Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC) and Commodity Futures Trading Commission (CFTC) respectively, the industry is watching closely for the next steps in what could become the most significant crypto legislation in US history.\"\n}\n```\n\nProcess exited with code 0."}],"details":{"status":"completed","sessionId":"glow-coral","exitCode":0,"aggregated":"I'll follow my SOUL.md instructions to fetch RSS feeds, cross-reference crypto news, and find the single biggest story. Let me start by gathering feeds from multiple sources.\nNow I can see the cross-feed pattern. The **CLARITY Act passing the Senate Banking Committee** is clearly the biggest story—covered by CoinTelegraph, Decrypt, CoinDesk, Reuters, CNBC, Bloomberg, and Google News sources. This is major regulatory news with bipartisan implications for the US crypto industry.\n\nLet me extract the specific URLs and check the history database to ensure this hasn't been published before:\n<reasoning>Both URLs return NOT_FOUND, meaning this topic hasn't been published before. I can proceed with this story. Now I need to extract the full article content from these sources using trafilatura. Let me fetch both articles.</reasoning>\nBoth URLs are fresh (NOT_FOUND). Now let me extract the full article content from both sources:\nI have successfully gathered comprehensive content about the CLARITY Act from both CoinTelegraph and Decrypt. The articles provide rich details about the Senate Banking Committee's vote, the legislative nuances, and the political dynamics surrounding this significant cryptocurrency regulation.\n\nI'll develop a structured JSON summary that captures the key insights: the bill's progression, the 15-9 committee vote, bipartisan tensions around ethics and amendments, and the potential implications for cryptocurrency regulation. The summary will highlight the critical role of Senators like Ruben Gallego and Angela Alsobrooks, the procedural maneuvers by Chairman Tim Scott, and the broader context of the bill's path to potential presidential signature.\n\nThe narrative will emphasize the bill's significance as the most substantial crypto regulatory legislation currently moving through Congress, with a focus on the complex political negotiations and the specific challenges it faces before reaching a full Senate vote.  \n\nI have extracted the full article content from both sources. Now let me compile the final aggregated JSON.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"topic_theme\": \"US Senate Advances CLARITY Act in Landmark Crypto Regulation Vote\",\n  \"primary_keyword\": \"CLARITY Act crypto\",\n  \"primary_headline\": \"US Senate Banking Committee votes to advance landmark CLARITY Act crypto regulation bill to full Senate floor\",\n  \"sources_used\": [\n    \"CoinTelegraph\",\n    \"Decrypt\"\n  ],\n  \"source_urls\": [\n    \"https://cointelegraph.com/news/us-senate-banking-committee-advance-clarity-act\",\n    \"https://decrypt.co/367871/democrats-split-clarity-act-crypto-bill-passes-senate-committee-vote\"\n  ],\n  \"combined_key_facts\": [\n    \"The Senate Banking Committee voted to advance the CLARITY Act, with the vote passing 15-9 along largely partisan lines\",\n    \"Two Democratic senators crossed party lines: Ruben Gallego (D-AZ) and Angela Alsobrooks (D-MD) voted in favor, despite earlier saying they would not vote yes without a deal on limiting President Trump's personal crypto ventures\",\n    \"All 13 Republican committee members voted to advance the bill, which now moves to the full Senate floor for final consideration\",\n    \"The bill needs 60 votes to pass the Senate, meaning it will require support from at least 7 Democrats on the floor vote\",\n    \"If passed and signed by President Trump, the CLARITY Act would formally legalize most crypto activity in the United States\",\n    \"Senator Elizabeth Warren led Democratic opposition, proposing amendments on national security, money laundering, and reporting requirements related to Jeffrey Epstein's crypto connections\",\n    \"Senator Jack Reed criticized the bill as not truly bipartisan, accusing Chairman Tim Scott of 'arbitrarily' dismissing Democratic amendments\",\n    \"Procedural drama defined the hearing: Chairman Tim Scott refused to hold votes on certain amendments he claimed had 'technical drafting errors,' yet proceeded with other amendments having the same issues\",\n    \"Democratic amendments that failed included: Catherine Cortez Masto's proposal for more law enforcement authority over crypto cases, and Tina Smith's amendment to prohibit federal bailouts of crypto companies\",\n    \"Crypto markets reacted positively: Bitcoin rallied to $82,000, Coinbase shares surged 10%, and crypto stocks broadly gained on the regulatory clarity hopes\",\n    \"Stand With Crypto, a Coinbase-backed advocacy group, announced it would score lawmakers based on this vote and integrate these scores into future election endorsements\",\n    \"The Solana Policy Institute expects the House will likely pass identical language if it clears the Senate, sending it directly to the President's desk\"\n  ],\n  \"aggregated_raw_content\": \"US Senate Banking Committee Votes to Advance CLARITY Act: Landmark Crypto Regulation Moves to Full Senate\\n\\nThe US Senate Banking Committee voted Thursday to advance the CLARITY Act, a landmark piece of cryptocurrency legislation that would formally legalize most crypto activity in the United States. The vote passed largely along partisan lines, 13 Republicans in favor, with Democrats splintered on the legislation.\\n\\nSenate Democrats Divided on Landmark Bill\\n\\nSenate Democrats splintered Thursday in their support of the CLARITY Act during the crucial committee vote. While most Democrats on the committee opposed the bill, two pro-crypto members of the party voted in favor: Senator Ruben Gallego (D-AZ) and Senator Angela Alsobrooks (D-MD). Both had previously indicated they would not vote for the legislation unless a deal on language related to limiting President Donald Trump's personal crypto ventures was reached. No such deal had been finalized by the time of the vote.\\n\\nWhen reached after the vote, an Alsobrooks spokesperson told Decrypt the senator voted yes \\\"because of the good faith negotiations that have transpired,\\\" and added the senator \\\"will continue negotiating on ethics specifically.\\\" At the hearing, Gallego qualified his \\\"yes\\\" vote by saying it may change in the future if an ethics deal regarding the president isn't reached. \\\"We have come close, but not finished, an agreement on ethics guardrails for elected officials—all elected officials,\\\" Gallego said. \\\"If this is not resolved by the time of the floor [vote], like I have in the past, I am not afraid to vote no,\\\" he added.\\n\\nDemocratic opposition was led by Senator Elizabeth Warren and Senator Jack Reed. Warren proposed multiple amendments, including provisions on sandboxes for AI and ones on \\\"tokenization loopholes\\\" and money laundering, citing reports that Iran was collecting tolls in crypto for ships using the Strait of Hormuz. Another Warren amendment demanded US banking regulators report on information related to deceased sex offender Jeffrey Epstein, whom she described as an \\\"early backer of crypto.\\\" Senator Cynthia Lummis, a Republican advocate for the bill, argued the Epstein provision was not related to digital assets. Lawmakers voted along party lines, and the amendment failed to pass.\\n\\nSenator Jack Reed criticized the bill as not representing true bipartisan work, given that Chairman Scott had \\\"arbitrarily\\\" dismissed consideration of amendments Democrats had proposed. Warren echoed concerns about the administration, proposing an amendment to continue funding the Consumer Financial Protection Bureau in response to attempts to shutter the agency since 2025.\\n\\nProcedural Drama and Failed Amendments\\n\\nThe hearing was marked by procedural tension. Chairman Tim Scott (R-SC) refused to hold votes on certain amendments to the CLARITY Act that would have proven uncomfortable for Republican colleagues. One such amendment would have forced lawmakers to choose sides in a feud between the banking lobby and the crypto industry over yield on stablecoins. Another would have likely seen Republicans vote down changes to the bill's provisions on decentralized finance requested by law enforcement.\\n\\nScott claimed he was preventing those votes due to a technical drafting error—but then proceeded to hold a vote on other amendments with the same drafting issues. \\\"I am deeply concerned that law enforcement officials… [and] community banks will listen to this and wonder why their point of view just couldn't be heard,\\\" Elizabeth Warren said during the skirmish.\\n\\nSenator Catherine Cortez Masto, a Democrat who expressed general support for the CLARITY Act at the markup, introduced an amendment that would give law enforcement more authority over crypto-related cases. The amendment failed along party lines. Democratic Senator Tina Smith proposed an amendment to prohibit federal agencies from bailing out crypto companies if another market crash were to occur. Calling it a \\\"preventative measure\\\" in response to market volatility, all Democrats voted in favor, but the amendment failed along party lines.\\n\\nSenator Chris Van Hollen proposed an amendment concerning Trump's potential conflicts of interest with the crypto industry through his family's World Liberty Financial business and memecoins. Republican Senator Bernie Moreno and Scott defended the president, accusing Van Hollen of \\\"ad hominem\\\" attacks. All 13 Republicans voted against the provision. \\\"The people involved directly in making these policies, from the president to the Congress, should not be able to be issuers of these particular assets and coins,\\\" said Van Hollen. Senator Raphael Warnock withdrew an amendment in response to what he called \\\"pure corruption\\\" by the Trump administration, adding that he would not support any bill without these carveouts.\\n\\nRepublicans voted as a bloc against Democratic amendments on stablecoins and digital dollars proposed by Reed.\\n\\nMarket Reaction and Industry Response\\n\\nThe crypto market reacted positively to the committee vote. Bitcoin rallied to approximately $82,000 following the news. Coinbase shares surged approximately 10%, Strategy (formerly MicroStrategy) rallied 7%, and crypto stocks broadly gained on hopes of regulatory clarity.\\n\\nStand With Crypto, a Coinbase-backed crypto advocacy organization, announced it would score lawmakers based on the committee vote and incorporate these scores into future evaluations. \\\"Stand With Crypto scored senators' votes on the Banking Committee markup and will incorporate these into lawmakers' scores… so that every voter can understand where elected officials stand on crypto priorities,\\\" the organization said. Coinbase is a key backer of Fairshake, a crypto super PAC that plans to spend over $100 million on the November midterm elections.\\n\\nPath Forward\\n\\nThe CLARITY Act will now head to the full Senate floor for final consideration. If passed there, the bill will then require approval from the House of Representatives. \\\"I think it's so difficult to get Senate floor time, and if they get something through the Senate that has the votes, I think the House will probably pass that identical language, and then it will be able to go on to the president's desk for a signature,\\\" Solana Policy Institute President Kristin Smith told Cointelegraph before the markup.\\n\\nThe bill will need 60 votes to pass the Senate, meaning Republicans will need support from at least 7 Democrats on the floor vote. With the advancement of CLARITY in both the banking and agriculture committees to address laws and regulations under the Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC) and Commodity Futures Trading Commission (CFTC) respectively, the industry is watching closely for the next steps in what could become the most significant crypto legislation in US history.\"\n}\n```\n","name":"openclaw agent"},"isError":false,"timestamp":1778823681374}}
{"type":"message","id":"70ff3c9c","parentId":"4ce28cd3","timestamp":"2026-05-15T05:41:38.201Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":" ✅ **Research done, starting article...**\n\nThe researcher has found the biggest story: **The CLARITY Act passing the Senate Banking Committee** — a landmark crypto regulation bill that would legalize most crypto activity in the US. The vote was 15-9 with two Democrats crossing party lines.\n\nNow starting **Step 2: Write**"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"functions.exec:5","name":"exec","arguments":{"command":"rm -f ~/.openclaw/agents/writer/sessions/sessions.json && openclaw agent --agent writer --message \"First, read the COINOGRAPHY_TEMPLATE.md file in your workspace to learn your strict formatting rules. Then, write a 1100-1200 word crypto news article using these facts: {'topic_theme': 'US Senate Advances CLARITY Act in Landmark Crypto Regulation Vote', 'primary_keyword': 'CLARITY Act crypto', 'primary_headline': 'US Senate Banking Committee votes to advance landmark CLARITY Act crypto regulation bill to full Senate floor', 'sources_used': ['CoinTelegraph', 'Decrypt'], 'source_urls': ['https://cointelegraph.com/news/us-senate-banking-committee-advance-clarity-act', 'https://decrypt.co/367871/democrats-split-clarity-act-crypto-bill-passes-senate-committee-vote'], 'combined_key_facts': ['The Senate Banking Committee voted to advance the CLARITY Act, with the vote passing 15-9 along largely partisan lines', 'Two Democratic senators crossed party lines: Ruben Gallego (D-AZ) and Angela Alsobrooks (D-MD) voted in favor, despite earlier saying they would not vote yes without a deal on limiting President Trump\\'s personal crypto ventures', 'All 13 Republican committee members voted to advance the bill, which now moves to the full Senate floor for final consideration', 'The bill needs 60 votes to pass the Senate, meaning it will require support from at least 7 Democrats on the floor vote', 'If passed and signed by President Trump, the CLARITY Act would formally legalize most crypto activity in the United States', 'Senator Elizabeth Warren led Democratic opposition, proposing amendments on national security, money laundering, and reporting requirements related to Jeffrey Epstein\\'s crypto connections', 'Senator Jack Reed criticized the bill as not truly bipartisan, accusing Chairman Tim Scott of \\'arbitrarily\\' dismissing Democratic amendments', 'Procedural drama defined the hearing: Chairman Tim Scott refused to hold votes on certain amendments he claimed had \\'technical drafting errors,\\' yet proceeded with other amendments having the same issues', 'Democratic amendments that failed included: Catherine Cortez Masto\\'s proposal for more law enforcement authority over crypto cases, and Tina Smith\\'s amendment to prohibit federal bailouts of crypto companies', 'Crypto markets reacted positively: Bitcoin rallied to $82,000, Coinbase shares surged 10%, and crypto stocks broadly gained on the regulatory clarity hopes', 'Stand With Crypto, a Coinbase-backed advocacy group, announced it would score lawmakers based on this vote and integrate these scores into future election endorsements', 'The Solana Policy Institute expects the House will likely pass identical language if it clears the Senate, sending it directly to the President\\'s desk']}. You MUST finish the article completely, ending with the Sources and Word Count block.\" --deliver > /tmp/crypto-article.md","yieldMs":20000}}],"api":"bedrock-converse-stream","provider":"amazon-bedrock","model":"moonshotai.kimi-k2.5","usage":{"input":12040,"output":712,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":12752,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1778823681375}}
